-- title:
--   Width transport along both degeneracy maps, characteristic ≥ 5
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $M, s, q'$ with $M, s$ nonzero, $s$ prime, $q'$ prime with $5 \le q'$, $s \neq q'$ and $q' \nmid M$, and let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q'$, taken in the smallest universe. Write $F_N \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}(k)$ for the $k$-subfield `modularFunctionFieldC k N` generated by the $q$-series $j =$ `jqModC k` and by its $N$-fold substitution $q \mapsto q^N$, and let `jGeomGen k N` denote $j$ viewed in $F_N$. The assertion is: for every pair $\varphi_0, \varphi_1$ of $k$-algebra homomorphisms $F_M \to F_{Ms}$ (indexed by `Fin 2`) such that each $\varphi_i$ makes $F_{Ms}$ integral over $F_M$, such that $\varphi_0$ is the identity on underlying Laurent series and $\varphi_1$ acts as the substitution `qExpand k s` ($q \mapsto q^s$), for every $i \in \{0,1\}$ and every place $p$ of $F_{Ms}$ over $k$ (a proper valuation subring containing $k$ and a principal ideal ring) satisfying the divisibility $r_{Ms}(p) \mid W(j(p))$, one has $$e_{\varphi_i}(p) \cdot \mathrm{width}_{Ms}(p) = \mathrm{width}_M\bigl(\varphi_i^{*}p\bigr).$$ Here $j(p)$ is `Place.evalAt` of `jGeomGen` at $p$ (the residue of $j$, pulled back to $k$), $r_N(w)$ is the order of vanishing at $w$ of $j - j(w)$, $W(x) = 3, 2, 1$ according as $x = 0$, $x = 1728$, or otherwise, $\mathrm{width}_N(w) = W(j(w))/r_N(w)$ in natural-number division, $e_{\varphi_i}(p)$ is the ramification index of $p$ for the algebra structure on $F_{Ms}$ given by $\varphi_i$, and $\varphi_i^{*}p$ is the place of $F_M$ obtained by pulling the valuation subring back along $\varphi_i$.
--
--   This is the transport of automorphism widths along the two degeneracy embeddings $k(X_0(M)) \to k(X_0(Ms))$, in the form restricted to residue characteristic $q' \ge 5$ and to a coefficient field in the smallest universe; the divisibility hypothesis on the ramification over the $j$-line is what makes the natural-number division in $\mathrm{width}$ behave multiplicatively. It feeds the supersingular Hecke and degeneracy computations at level $Ms$, among them the compatibility of the degeneracy matrices with the Atkin–Lehner permutation and with the vertex and edge Hecke operators, and the Riemann–Roch estimate for traces along $\varphi_1$.
